            {
              "id": "glog-d7ee40e1-c23e-4f0b-93f3-784b4f286316",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, and denial of service.\n\nIn the given code snippet `malloc(n*sizeof(Monomer))`, the vulnerability arises if the variable `n` is controlled by the user or an attacker. If `n` is a very large value, it can cause the program to allocate more memory than what is available, leading to a denial of service. If `n` is a negative value, it can lead to memory corruption and potential execution of arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. Ensure that the size is not too large to cause a denial of service and not negative to prevent memory corruption. Also, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <limits.h>\n\n// ...\n\nif (n > 0 && n <= INT_MAX/sizeof(Monomer)) {\n    Monomer* monomers = (Monomer*) malloc(n*sizeof(Monomer));\n    if (monomers == NULL) {\n        // Handle memory allocation failure\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if `n` is a positive value and does not exceed the maximum value that can be allocated. Then, we check if the `malloc` function was successful in allocating the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- stdlib.h: This library is required for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
