            {
              "id": "glog-e9075b52-9ff0-4677-8ae8-8cbdc42d2ad2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the argument passed to `malloc` is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities, including buffer overflows, integer overflows, and memory corruption. In the provided sink, `malloc(*bytes)`, the argument `*bytes` is dereferenced without any checks, which can lead to undefined behavior if the pointer is null or points to an invalid memory location.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and control the arguments passed to `malloc`. This includes checking for null pointers, ensuring the size argument is not excessively large (which could cause an integer overflow), and ensuring the size argument is not zero (which is undefined behavior in C).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided sink:\n\n```c++\nif (bytes == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else if (*bytes <= 0 || *bytes > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(*bytes);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be a constant that represents the maximum reasonable size for an allocation in your program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
